# Enter Gödel’s Casino

A card’s logical status and its betting value are different questions. Begin with the one-card geometry, then build a deck, audit mistakes, and finally test the adversarial limit.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

## From one card to a deck

The threshold rule acts independently on every card, so a seemingly exponential strategy problem becomes a linear scan.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact-value theorem and proof idea</summary>
For marginal truth probabilities $q_1,\ldots,q_n$, predicting the more likely truth on card $i$ earns $|2q_i-1|$. Every alternative earns its negative. Summing gives the exact optimum $\sum_i|2q_i-1|$; independence is unnecessary because expectation is linear.
</details>

## What does a mistake cost?

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the regret identity</summary>
Agreement with the threshold prediction costs zero. Disagreement changes $|2q_i-1|$ to $-|2q_i-1|$, so its exact cost is $2|2q_i-1|$. Summing only over disagreements gives total regret.
</details>

## The adversarial mirror

Randomization cannot guarantee a strict gain in all worlds, because reversing every truth reverses every payoff.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the no-free-lunch proof</summary>
Pair any world $t$ with its complement $ ar t$. The mixed payoffs are $M(t)$ and $-M(t)$, so one is nonpositive. This works for every finite rational weighting of deterministic strategies.
</details>

## Continue exploring

Read about [Bayesian decision theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayes_estimator), [regret in decision theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regret_(decision_theory)), and [Gödel’s incompleteness theorems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6del%27s_incompleteness_theorems). The key discipline is to keep semantic truth, derivability, probability, and payoff conceptually separate.
